WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

1
00:00:01.040 --> 00:00:04.430
You already know that the world of
biology is very different if you look

2
00:00:04.430 --> 00:00:08.730
at something from far away, like the
body of a dog, than if you look up

3
00:00:08.730 --> 00:00:13.350
close and see organs, tissues, and
cells. Each different level of

4
00:00:13.350 --> 00:00:18.140
magnification reveals new features,
from the dog's fur color and size, to

5
00:00:18.140 --> 00:00:21.790
the way the cells are organized in its
body. If we keep zooming in, we'll

6
00:00:21.790 --> 00:00:26.240
start to see that, in fact, everything
is built of the same few basic building

7
00:00:26.240 --> 00:00:30.360
blocks, which join together in
complicated ways to form larger

8
00:00:30.360 --> 00:00:34.370
structures that eventually build the
cuddly dog you can recognize with the

9
00:00:34.370 --> 00:00:39.430
naked eye. Let's take a look into the
very smallest levels, the small and

10
00:00:39.430 --> 00:00:42.820
large molecules of the cell, to
understand what these building blocks

11
00:00:42.830 --> 00:00:47.570
are and how they come together. Let's
start small with what are referred to

12
00:00:47.570 --> 00:00:53.120
as small molecules or monomers. These
have important function on their own,

13
00:00:53.130 --> 00:00:58.720
but can also link up like Lego pieces
to form large or macromolecules. There

14
00:00:58.720 --> 00:01:03.550
are four main categories of small
molecules: nucleic acids, simple sugars

15
00:01:03.560 --> 00:01:09.010
(such as glucose), fatty acids, and
amino acids. Nucleic acids are made up

16
00:01:09.020 --> 00:01:14.080
of a sugar backbone and a base. The
base is the monomer. There are four of

17
00:01:14.090 --> 00:01:19.520
these bases: adenine, guanine, cytosine
and thymine. You have probably heard of

18
00:01:19.520 --> 00:01:24.780
these as the building blocks of DNA and
the related nucleic acid, RNA. RNA

19
00:01:24.790 --> 00:01:29.510
actually uses uracil instead of
thymine. The smaller nucleotides also

20
00:01:29.510 --> 00:01:33.950
have critical roles in our cells. One
of them, adenosine, which is formed

21
00:01:33.960 --> 00:01:39.140
when adenine binds to ribose sugar, can
be modified to form a molecule called

22
00:01:39.150 --> 00:01:45.220
ATP, or adenosine triphosphate. ATP is
the most important energy source for

23
00:01:45.220 --> 00:01:50.200
our cells. Now let's move on to the
simple sugars, for example, glucose,

24
00:01:50.200 --> 00:01:55.030
fructose, and galactose. When two of
these sugar monomers join up, they form

25
00:01:55.030 --> 00:01:59.580
what is called a disaccharide. One
example of this is lactose, found in

26
00:01:59.580 --> 00:02:04.160
milk, which contains one glucose
molecule and one galactose molecule.

27
00:02:04.440 --> 00:02:09.020
Mono and disaccharides can be broken
down all the way to carbon dioxide,

28
00:02:09.240 --> 00:02:13.600
generating a huge amount of energy
along the way. If you've ever had a

29
00:02:13.600 --> 00:02:16.950
sugar rush before, you won't be
surprised that these are the main

30
00:02:16.950 --> 00:02:21.730
sources of energy for the cell.
Breaking down one glucose molecule

31
00:02:21.730 --> 00:02:27.450
produces about 30 of those energy rich
ATP molecules. Once cells use up their

32
00:02:27.450 --> 00:02:32.140
glucose stores, they turn to breaking
down fatty acids, another type of small

33
00:02:32.140 --> 00:02:37.410
molecule. Fatty acids actually store
two times as much energy as the same

34
00:02:37.410 --> 00:02:41.680
massive sugars, which means that
breaking them down leads to lots of ATP.

35
00:02:42.440 --> 00:02:46.560
Like sugars and fatty acids, amino
acids are another type of small

36
00:02:46.560 --> 00:02:51.510
molecule that can be further broken
down for use as fuel. But they have

37
00:02:51.510 --> 00:02:55.930
several other important functions as
well. You can think of amino acids as

38
00:02:55.930 --> 00:03:00.210
the Swiss Army knife of the small
molecules. Each has the same basic

39
00:03:00.210 --> 00:03:04.640
structure, but then has one of 20
possible attachments coming off of the

40
00:03:04.640 --> 00:03:08.480
main structure. Each attachment has
different properties, so different

41
00:03:08.490 --> 00:03:13.790
amino acids can be used for diverse
roles. For example, glycine can sense

42
00:03:13.790 --> 00:03:18.970
signals in the brain. Glutamine can
also do this and can be converted to

43
00:03:18.970 --> 00:03:24.590
glutamate to make food taste delicious.
But why do cells need so much energy? A

44
00:03:24.590 --> 00:03:28.970
major reason is that they need to build
these small molecules into larger

45
00:03:29.480 --> 00:03:33.360
macromolecules. First, let's look at
how macromolecules are usually

46
00:03:33.360 --> 00:03:38.530
assembled from the monomers. The
individual small molecule units can

47
00:03:38.540 --> 00:03:43.570
only attach to another monomer in two
places. Stringing several monomers

48
00:03:43.570 --> 00:03:48.830
together end to end gives a polymer, or
large macromolecule. We'll next talk

49
00:03:48.830 --> 00:03:53.240
about the four main types of
macromolecules: nucleic acids, complex

50
00:03:53.240 --> 00:03:58.410
carbohydrates, proteins, and lipids. As
we mentioned, nucleotides can string

51
00:03:58.410 --> 00:04:02.710
together to form nucleic acids, which
come in two types— DNA

52
00:04:02.720 --> 00:04:08.510
(deoxyribonucleic acid), which stores
are genes, and RNA (ribonucleic acid),

53
00:04:08.520 --> 00:04:13.160
which has many functions including
acting as a messenger between the DNA

54
00:04:13.160 --> 00:04:18.920
code and the production of proteins.
Let's move on to sugars. Our cells take

55
00:04:18.920 --> 00:04:23.500
the simple sugars, like glucose, from
our food and build them into complex

56
00:04:23.500 --> 00:04:28.700
carbohydrates that can be stored. Human
cells store carbohydrates as glycogen,

57
00:04:28.710 --> 00:04:33.580
plant cells as starch. We can also
directly take in complex carbohydrates

58
00:04:33.580 --> 00:04:38.590
from our diet. These are abundant in
foods like bread and potatoes. Those

59
00:04:38.590 --> 00:04:43.370
get broken down and then are either
directly used for energy or are built

60
00:04:43.380 --> 00:04:48.590
back up into glycogen for long term
storage. The process is dynamic. Amino

61
00:04:48.600 --> 00:04:53.530
acid monomers join directly together to
form proteins. As we mentioned, there

62
00:04:53.530 --> 00:04:58.550
are 20 different amino acids. These can
link up in many different orders and in

63
00:04:58.550 --> 00:05:02.590
chains of varying length, which means
that we can make many thousands of

64
00:05:02.600 --> 00:05:06.750
different types of proteins. These
proteins are used for cell structure,

65
00:05:06.760 --> 00:05:12.520
to carry out chemical reactions inside
cells, and many other functions. We

66
00:05:12.520 --> 00:05:16.530
saved lipids for last because they are
a little unique. To make lipids, fatty

67
00:05:16.540 --> 00:05:21.100
acids do not join up in a line like the
other types of monomers. There are

68
00:05:21.100 --> 00:05:25.720
several types of lipids. One type is
called a triglyceride, where three

69
00:05:25.720 --> 00:05:30.670
fatty acid monomers join up along a
glycerol molecule. These are the main

70
00:05:30.670 --> 00:05:36.500
way our bodies store energy in the form
of fat. Two fatty acids joined to a

71
00:05:36.510 --> 00:05:41.590
phosphate group forms a phospholipid,
which is the main component of our cell

72
00:05:41.590 --> 00:05:47.000
membranes. You can see that our cells
use molecules of a wide range of sizes.

73
00:05:47.010 --> 00:05:51.700
We can get a lot of these from our diet.
A big glass of whole milk contains

74
00:05:51.700 --> 00:05:57.080
calcium (an atom), lactose (a
disaccharide or simple sugar), proteins

75
00:05:57.080 --> 00:06:01.530
and fats. Our cells can take the
simpler molecules and use them for

76
00:06:01.540 --> 00:06:06.420
energy right away or can break them
down into smaller pieces. Just like

77
00:06:06.420 --> 00:06:10.710
when you disassemble a Lego house
you've made, the individual Lego pieces

78
00:06:10.720 --> 00:06:15.580
can then be used in a whole new
construction. For our cells, this means

79
00:06:15.580 --> 00:06:21.000
assembling new macromolecules. Overall,
this dynamic cycle of breaking down

80
00:06:21.000 --> 00:06:26.790
and building up macromolecules powers
our cells, tissues, organs— our bodies

81
00:06:26.790 --> 00:06:27.460
as a whole.

